= i
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

We take $d\in\mathbb Q^\times$, as required for this rational <elliptic curve>. The tangent at $O=(1:-1:0)$ is $X+Y=0$. Its intersection with the curve has $dZ^3=0$, so it meets the curve three times at $O$: the identity is an <inflection point of a plane cubic>.

For $P,Q$, let their chord meet the <diagonal cubic elliptic curve> at the third point $R$, using the tangent if $P=Q$ and counting <intersection multiplicities>. Then join $R$ to $O$ and take the third intersection $S$. Define $P+Q=S$. Equivalently, three collinear intersection points satisfy $P+Q+R=O$.

Here inversion has the particularly simple form
$$
\boxed{-(X:Y:Z)=(Y:X:Z).}
$$
Indeed $P$, its displayed inverse, and $O$ lie on the same line, since swapping $X,Y$ preserves both the curve and $X+Y$. Thus **addition is the chord or tangent operation followed by swapping the first two coordinates**. The rule includes limiting cases involving $O$ and repeated intersections. Its <associativity> follows from the <elliptic curve group law from Riemann-Roch>: the identification $P\mapsto[P-O]$ with the degree-zero <Picard group> turns the line-intersection <Weil divisor> relation into addition of <divisor classes>. Since a line cuts a <Weil divisor> linearly equivalent to $3O$, that relation is exactly the chord rule just described.

= ii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <Hessian criterion for flexes of a plane cubic> applied to the defining <polynomial> gives
$$
\det\begin{pmatrix}6X&0&0\\0&6Y&0\\0&0&6dZ\end{pmatrix}=216dXYZ.
$$
Consequently the <inflection points of a plane cubic> on this curve are exactly its intersections with the three coordinate lines. Choose $r\in\overline{\mathbb Q}$ with $r^3=d$, and a primitive cube <root of unity> $\zeta$. The nine distinct <flexes of a diagonal cubic> are
$$
\boxed{(1:-\zeta^j:0),\quad(0:-r\zeta^j:1),\quad(-r\zeta^j:0:1),\qquad j=0,1,2.}
$$
With the chosen flex as identity, these points are precisely $E_d[3]$. A tangent cutting the <Weil divisor> $3P$ gives $3(P-O)=0$; conversely $3(P-O)=0$ makes the hyperplane <Weil divisor> equivalent to $3P$, which supplies a tangent with triple contact. This is the <three-torsion points are flexes of a plane cubic> correspondence.

On $Z=0$ only $O$ is rational, since the other cube <roots of unity> are not rational. On each other coordinate line a <rational point> exists exactly when $d$ is a rational cube, and then exactly one does. Therefore
$$
\boxed{E_d(\mathbb Q)[3]\cong\begin{cases}\mathbb Z/3\mathbb Z,&d\in(\mathbb Q^\times)^3,\\0,&\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}}
$$
In particular the rational <torsion subgroup> cannot contain two independent points of order $3$.

One can also determine the whole <rational torsion of a diagonal cubic>. First it is killed by $6$. To see this, use the <Weierstrass equation of an elliptic curve> from (iii). At <primes> $\ell\equiv2\pmod3$ outside the finite set containing $2,3$ and the prime divisors of the numerator and denominator of $d$, the cube map permutes $\mathbb F_\ell$, so
$$
\#E_d(\mathbb F_\ell)=1+\sum_{x\in\mathbb F_\ell}\bigl(1+\chi(x^3-432d^2)\bigr)=\ell+1,
$$
where the quadratic character is extended by $\chi(0)=0$, and the character sum vanishes after substituting $t=x^3-432d^2$. <Reduction of torsion points on an elliptic curve> injects the prime-to-$\ell$ part of rational torsion into this <finite group>. This injection follows because multiplication by an <integer> <prime> to $\ell$ is invertible on the <formal group of an elliptic curve>, and hence its reduction kernel has no such torsion.

For any <prime> $q>3$, choose a good $\ell\equiv2\pmod3$ with $\ell\equiv1\pmod q$; the <Dirichlet theorem on primes in arithmetic progressions> and the <Chinese remainder theorem> supply such <primes> outside the finite bad <set>. Then $q\nmid\ell+1$, excluding $q$-torsion. Similarly good $\ell\equiv5\pmod{12}$ exclude order $4$, and good $\ell\equiv11\pmod{18}$ exclude order $9$. Thus every rational torsion point has order dividing $6$.

The inversion formula in (i) shows that the only possible nonidentity <rational point> of order $2$ has $X=Y$ and $Z\ne0$. It exists precisely when $(X/Z)^3=-d/2$, that is, when $d/2$ is a rational cube. There is at most one such point. This condition and $d$ being a cube cannot hold together, since $2$ is not a rational cube. Combining it with the three-torsion calculation gives the full answer:
$$
\boxed{E_d(\mathbb Q)_{\mathrm{tors}}\cong\begin{cases}
\mathbb Z/3\mathbb Z,&d\in(\mathbb Q^\times)^3,\\
\mathbb Z/2\mathbb Z,&d/2\in(\mathbb Q^\times)^3,\\
0,&\text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}}
$$

= iii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Use the invertible linear <projective transformation>
$$
(\mathcal X:\mathcal Y:\mathcal Z)=(-12dZ:-36d(X-Y):X+Y).
$$
The identity
$$
(X+Y)^3+3(X+Y)(X-Y)^2=4(X^3+Y^3)=-4dZ^3
$$
gives
$$
\mathcal Y^2\mathcal Z=1296d^2(X-Y)^2(X+Y)=\mathcal X^3-432d^2\mathcal Z^3.
$$
The transformation is invertible because $d\ne0$, and it sends $O$ to $(0:1:0)$. Hence it is an <isomorphism> of the pointed <smooth projective curves>, giving the required <Weierstrass equation of an elliptic curve>:
$$
\boxed{y^2=x^3-432d^2.}
$$
For affine points $u=X/Z,v=Y/Z$, the transformation and its inverse are
$$
x=-\frac{12d}{u+v},\qquad y=-\frac{36d(u-v)}{u+v},\qquad
u=\frac{y-36d}{6x},\qquad v=\frac{-y-36d}{6x}.
$$
These formulae are interpreted in their stated affine charts; the <projective transformation> handles the exceptional points without any ambiguity.

= iv
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Set $d=-7$. The affine rational pair $(u,v)=(2,-1)$ gives $P=(84,756)$ on the <elliptic curve> $y^2=x^3-21168$. It is a point of infinite order: in the <rational torsion of a diagonal cubic> classification, neither $-7$ nor $-7/2$ is a rational cube, so this curve has trivial rational <torsion subgroup>.

There is also a short direct torsion check specific to this curve. The <primes> $5$ and $11$ are good and have point counts $6$ and $12$, respectively, because cubing is bijective in their <finite fields>. Reduction at these two <primes> bounds the entire rational torsion by order dividing $6$, including its possible $5$- and $11$-primary parts by reduction at the other <prime>. The flex calculation excludes order $3$, and the inversion calculation excludes order $2$. Hence $P$ is indeed nontorsion.

Every nonzero multiple $nP$ is distinct, and its inverse projective image has $Z\ne0$: the only <rational point> at infinity on the diagonal cubic is its identity. Thus each yields a distinct rational pair. More explicitly, if $nP=(x_n,y_n)$ on the <Weierstrass model>, then
$$
\boxed{u_n=\frac{y_n+252}{6x_n},\qquad v_n=\frac{252-y_n}{6x_n},\qquad u_n^3+v_n^3=7\quad(n\ge1).}
$$
Here $x_n\ne0$, since $x_n=0$ would require a rational square equal to $-21168$. For example the <elliptic-curve addition formula> gives $2P=(28,28)$, which produces $(u_2,v_2)=(5/3,4/3)$. This proves **there are infinitely many rational pairs of cubes summing to seven**.

